True but these things are always about potential liability, if you get an illegal request like this the first thing you should do is respond ***in writing*** saying hey you asked me to do this, here's the state code where I think this is an illegal request, so I'm not going to do it. 

&amp;#x200B;

Then if you're fired for "no reason" or any other weakly supported reason you'd have a good chance to sue under this law. That state in particular is out for the employee by putting a shit ton of fees that compound if the employer is found to have broken the law, that way their potential damages isn't a few months worth of work at minimum wage or whatever. 

&amp;#x200B;

That being said if they find a legitimate reason to fire you then well having a natural hair style, or having an employer that had to be corrected isn't a get out of jail free card. You can't just not show up to work and then claim it was about your hairstyle. 

